<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later

  PGN Studio-authored Win32 application manifest for the pgn-extract
  sidecar (architecture.md §10.2, Phase 0b Unicode-path requirement).
  This file is our own, not upstream pgn-extract content, and is embedded
  into the built binary by scripts/build-pgn-extract.ps1 via
  `link.exe /MANIFEST:EMBED /MANIFESTINPUT:<this file>`.

  Why this exists: pgn-extract opens files with the ANSI CRT functions
  fopen()/access() (see hashing.c, lex.c). On Windows, "ANSI" means
  "bytes interpreted using the process's active code page" (CP_ACP).
  Historically CP_ACP is a legacy code page (e.g. Windows-1252) that
  cannot represent most non-Latin scripts, so any path containing e.g.
  Bengali characters gets mangled to '?' before it ever reaches
  CreateFileW, and the open fails.

  Windows 10 1903+ lets a process opt in to UTF-8 as its ANSI code page
  via this manifest setting (see Microsoft's "Use UTF-8 code pages in
  Windows apps" docs: ActiveCodePage / <ws2:activeCodePage>). With this
  manifest embedded, GetACP() returns CP_UTF8 (65001) for the whole
  process, so fopen()/access() (and the CRT's own construction of argv
  from the OS's wide command line at startup) all correctly round-trip
  UTF-8 byte sequences instead of mangling them.

  Empirically verified (Phase 0b scratch probe) against
  fixtures/unicode-paths/: WITHOUT this manifest, pgn-extract fails with
  "Unable to find <mangled-path-with-????>" and exits 1. WITH this
  manifest embedded, it correctly opens and processes the same file,
  including a Bengali-script PGN comment inside it.

  Do not remove the <trustInfo> block the linker auto-generates when
  merging this fragment - only <application>/<windowsSettings> is ours;
  requestedExecutionLevel="asInvoker" is link.exe's own default and is
  fine for a non-privileged CLI tool.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings xmlns:ws2="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">
      <ws2:activeCodePage>UTF-8</ws2:activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
